    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "I would like to paraphrase the problem in a more mathematical way.\n\nA library has six identical copies of a certain book. At any given time, some of these copies are at the library and some are checked out. We want to find out how many different ways there are for some of the books to be in the library and the rest to be checked out, with the condition that at least one book is in the library and at least one is checked out.\n\nTo solve this problem, we can use the concept of binomial coefficient. Let $x$ be the number of books kept in the library, and $y$ be the number of books checked out. We have 6 books in total, so $x + y = 6$.\n\nSince the books are indistinguishable, we need to find the number of ways to distribute the books between the library and the checked-out books. This is equivalent to finding the number of ways to choose $x$ books to be kept in the library out of the total 6 books.\n\nUsing the binomial coefficient $\\binom{n}{k}$ to find the number of ways to choose $k$ objects from $n$ distinct objects, the number of ways to distribute the books is given by $\\binom{6}{x}$. Since $x$ can take on values between 1 and 5 (because at least one book needs to be kept in the library and at least one needs to be checked out), we need to calculate the sum of $\\binom{6}{x}$ for $x$ from 1 to 5.\n\nCounting the number of different ways: $\\binom{6}{1} + \\binom{6}{2} + \\binom{6}{3} + \\binom{6}{4} + \\binom{6}{5} = 6 + 15 + 20 + 15 + 6 = \\boxed{52}$.",
